WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 284: Steve Jobs: The Garage in Los Altos, the Macintosh, and the iPhone Revolution

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.760
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:07.040
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:18.320
[Rebecca] Today, our top 50 famous non-president American series explores the digital renaissance and aesthetic perfectionism of the tech visionary who reshaped modern civilization.

4
00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:24.720
[Rebecca] Here is George with the extraordinary life of Steve Jobs.

5
00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:43.040
[George] Born in San Francisco in February 1955 to unwed graduate students Joanne Skiebel and Abdulfatta Jandali, Stephen Paul Jobs was adopted at birth by Paul and Clara Jobs, a working-class machinist and accountant who settled in the blossoming apricot orchards of Silicon Valley.

6
00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:48.560
[George] In his father's home workshop, young Steve learned the aesthetics of fine craftsmanship.

7
00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:57.040
[George] Paul taught him that the back of a wooden cabinet or fence must be crafted with as much beauty and care as the front, even if no one would ever see it.

8
00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:59.520
[George] A rebellious and precocious student.

9
00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:14.560
[George] Steve attended Reed College in Oregon for barely six months before dropping out, sleeping on friends' dorm room floors, returning coke bottles for nickel deposits, and auditing a calligraphy class that taught him about serif and sans serif typefaces.

10
00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:25.520
[George] In 1974, after a soul-searching spiritual pilgrimage to India, Jobs reconnected with his brilliant hacker friend Steve Wozniak at the homebrew computer club.

11
00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:42.320
[George] In April 1976, working out of the Jobs family garage on Chris Drive in Los Altos, 21-year-old Jobs and 25-year-old Wozniak founded Apple Computer, selling 50 Apple I circuit boards to the bite shop for $500 a piece.

12
00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:59.040
[George] His adoptive father Paul Jobs was a gentle, quiet giant who rebuilt salvaged automobiles in the family garage, giving young Steve his own workbench and showing him how to solder wires, sand wood, and appreciate the hidden structural craftsmanship of everyday objects.

13
00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:15.080
[George] At Homestead High School in Cupertino, Jobs was fascinated by electronics but repelled by the social cliques of conventional sports and student clubs, gravitating instead toward countercultural literature, Bob Dylan bootlegs, and Zen meditation.

14
00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:30.200
[George] When Jobs and Steve Wozniak collaborated with Captain Crunch, John Draper to manufacture and sell illegal, blue boxes that hacked ATT telephone switches to make free international calls, Jobs recognized an intoxicating truth.

15
00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:36.040
[George] That a tiny team of clever young rebels could defeat a multibillion dollar corporate monopoly.

16
00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:51.160
[George] While Wozniak was a pure engineering genius who designed the entire circuitry of the Apple II with minimalist elegance, Jobs possessed the visionary intuition that transformed a hobbyist circuit board into a consumer appliance for ordinary households.

17
00:02:51.160 --> 00:03:06.520
[George] Jobs insisted that the Apple II be housed in a sleek, molded beige plastic case with no visible screws, eliminated noisy cooling fans, and hired legendary publicist Regis McKenna to design the iconic Bitnapple Rainbow logo.

18
00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:13.800
[George] Unveiled at the West Coast Computer Fair in April 1977, the Apple II was an explosive triumph.

19
00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:24.280
[George] Powered by Dan Brickland's Vici Calc spreadsheet software, it ignited the personal computer revolution, turning Apple into the fastest growing company in American history.

20
00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:37.000
[George] When Apple went public in December 1980, 25 year old Jobs was worth over 250 million dollars, becoming the youthful, charismatic poster child of Silicon Valley innovation.

21
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:52.080
[George] The Apple II was the crucial bridge that transformed computers from hobbyist tinkering kits into friendly household appliances, featuring built-in keyboard input, elegant plastic injection molding, and groundbreaking color graphics capability.

22
00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:56.640
[George] Jobs' relentless aesthetic demands drove his engineers to distraction.

23
00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:13.120
[George] He rejected hundreds of shades of beige before selecting the exact muted tone for the casing, demanded that the interior circuit board have its chips aligned in straight, harmonious rows, and insisted that the machine operate in complete silence without noisy cooling fans.

24
00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:23.680
[George] When the Apple II became the corporate engine that carried Apple's historic initial public offering in December 1980, Jobs joined the ranks of the nation's wealthiest entrepreneurs.

25
00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:31.840
[George] Yet he continued to dress in Levi's jeans and Birkenstock sandals, viewing himself as an artist rather than a corporate executive.

26
00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:44.560
[George] In December 1979, Jobs visited the Xerox Palo Alto Research Center, PARC, in exchange for allowing Xerox to purchase 100,000 shares of pre-IPO Apple stock.

27
00:04:44.560 --> 00:05:01.680
[George] There, Jobs was struck like lightning by three experimental technologies: object-oriented programming, networked computers, and most crucially, a graphical user interface featuring bitmapped graphics, overlapping windows, and a handheld pointing device called a mouse.

28
00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:11.440
[George] Jobs was astonished that Xerox executives failed to realize the magnitude of what they had created, storming out shouting, Why aren't you doing anything with this?

29
00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:13.200
[George] This is the greatest thing.

30
00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:14.960
[George] This is revolutionary.

31
00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:18.720
[George] Jobs seized control of the Macintosh Project at Apple.

32
00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:31.560
[George] Driving his team of rebel engineers with what colleagues called his reality distortion field, a mesmerizing blend of charismatic will, relentless perfectionism, and refusal to accept technical limitations.

33
00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:46.440
[George] On January 24, 1984, Jobs introduced the Macintosh at Flint Center, demonstrating proportional fonts, mouse navigation, and synthesized speech in a machine that democratized personal computing forever.

34
00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:59.960
[George] Jobs' visit to Xerox Park in December 1979 was one of the legendary turning points of modern computing, while Xerox's corporate brass viewed the graphical user interface as an obscure academic novelty.

35
00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:09.880
[George] Jobs immediately grasped that bitmapped graphics and a mouse were the ultimate egalitarian breakthrough that would allow ordinary human beings to interact with digital minds.

36
00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:21.320
[George] Jobs drove the Macintosh team with ferocious intensity, flying a pirate flag above their Bantley 3 office building and declaring that it's better to be a pirate than to join the Navy.

37
00:06:21.320 --> 00:06:36.680
[George] When the iconic 1984 Super Bowl commercial, directed by Ridley Scott, aired before 90 million viewers, it shocked corporate America by portraying the Macintosh as a revolutionary hammer, shattering the Orwellian conformity of IBM.

38
00:06:36.680 --> 00:06:49.640
[George] Following sluggish Macintosh sales and deep philosophical clashes with Apple CEO John Scully, whom Jobs had lured from Pepsi with the famous challenge Do you want to sell sugar water for the rest of your life?

39
00:06:49.640 --> 00:06:52.520
[George] Or do you want to come with me and change the world?

40
00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:57.800
[George] The Apple board stripped Jobs of all operational authority in May 1985.

41
00:06:57.800 --> 00:07:05.880
[George] Devastated and publicly humiliated, 30-year old Jobs resigned, selling all his Apple stock except a single share.

42
00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:20.880
[George] Starting over, Jobs founded Knee XT Computer, building an elegant black magnesium workstation with advanced object-oriented software called Ni XT Step, the software architecture that Tim Berners-Lee used to create the World Wide Web.

43
00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:29.040
[George] Simultaneously, Jobs bought the computer graphics division of Lucasfilm for $10 million, christening it Pixar.

44
00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:32.960
[George] Pouring $50 million of his personal fortune into Pixar.

45
00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:48.560
[George] Jobs partnered with creative visionary John Lassiter to produce Toy Story in 1995, the world's first computer-animated feature film, scoring a monumental box office triumph and making Jobs an overnight billionaire when Pixar went public.

46
00:07:48.560 --> 00:08:03.200
[George] The boardroom showdown of May 1985 between Jobs and John Scully tore Apple apart, resulting in Jobs' total eviction from the very company he had created, an agonizing humiliation that plunged him into deep depression for months.

47
00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:07.760
[George] But his wilderness years proved to be an indispensable artistic crucible.

48
00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:14.560
[George] At NiXT, Jobs honed his obsession with object-oriented software and architectural simplicity.

49
00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:23.040
[George] While his $70 million investment in Pixar kept the struggling digital animation studio alive through a decade of financial losses.

50
00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:38.640
[George] Toy Story's historic triumph in 1995 demonstrated Jobs' peerless ability to identify creative genius in others, validating his belief that the intersection of technology and human storytelling was the most fertile frontier of modern culture.

51
00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:50.400
[George] By late 1996, Apple was within 90 days of total bankruptcy, losing hundreds of millions of dollars on mediocre clone computers and an obsolete operating system.

52
00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:59.520
[George] In desperation, Apple's CEO Gil Emilio acquired NiXT for $400 million dollars, bringing Jobs back as an informal advisor.

53
00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:08.760
[George] Within months, Jobs orchestrated a boardroom purge, ousted Emilio, and returned as interim CEO, earning a salary of $1 a year.

54
00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:16.600
[George] Jobs immediately slashed 70% of Apple's bloated product line, focusing exclusively on four core machines.

55
00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:30.600
[George] Partnering with visionary British industrial designer Joni Ive, Jobs launched the Bondi Blue iMac in 1998, featuring a translucent teardrop chassis that banished ugly beige boxes from consumer electronics.

56
00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:42.680
[George] Championed by the iconic, Think Different Advertising Campaign honoring the crazy ones, the misfits, and the troublemakers who push the human race forward, Apple was resurrected from the ashes.

57
00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:45.960
[George] Returning to a dying Apple in 1997.

58
00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:52.280
[George] Jobs staged a ruthless corporate restructuring that remains the gold standard of executive leadership.

59
00:09:52.280 --> 00:10:04.760
[George] He eliminated 30 product variants, terminated unprofitable software licensing deals, and focused Apple's finite resources on two consumer computers and two professional workstations.

60
00:10:04.760 --> 00:10:18.840
[George] The Think Different Advertising Campaign, voiced in an unreleased cut by Jobs himself, captured his personal ethos, honoring the rebels, the round pegs in the square holes, who see things differently and change the world.

61
00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:31.800
[George] His partnership with British design prodigy Joni Ive produced the candy-colored iMac, which sold millions of units and proved that consumer electronics could be desirable, joyous objects of industrial art.

62
00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:46.880
[George] In October 2001, Jobs introduced the iPod with the simple, breathtaking promise, a thousand songs in your pocket, paired with the iTunes Music Store, which persuaded record labels to sell digital songs for 99 cents.

63
00:10:47.120 --> 00:10:54.880
[George] Jobs single-handedly rescued the music industry from digital piracy and made Apple the supreme arbiter of digital culture.

64
00:10:54.880 --> 00:11:05.040
[George] Then, on January 9, 2007, at the Macworld Convention in San Francisco, Jobs delivered the greatest product keynote in corporate history.

65
00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:19.120
[George] Walking onto the stage, he teased the audience that Apple was introducing three revolutionary products: a widescreen iPod with touch controls, a revolutionary mobile phone, and a breakthrough Internet communications device.

66
00:11:19.120 --> 00:11:25.680
[George] Pausing for dramatic effect, he smiled and revealed, these are not three separate devices.

67
00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:27.360
[George] This is one device.

68
00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:29.920
[George] And we are calling it, iPhone.

69
00:11:29.920 --> 00:11:42.480
[George] Featuring multitouch capacitive glass, visual voicemail, and the app store, the iPhone transformed the modern world, putting the sum total of human knowledge and communication into the palms of billions.

70
00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:59.760
[George] The launch of the iPod in 2001 and the iTunes Music Store in 2003 fundamentally rescued the global music industry from the chaos of filesharing piracy, convincing major record labels to unbundle complete albums and sell individual songs for 99 cents.

71
00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:03.040
[George] But the iPhone was Jobs' supreme masterpiece.

72
00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:16.560
[George] He recognized that smartphones of the era were crippled by tiny plastic keyboards and clunky styluses, boldly replacing them with multitouch capacitive glass screens that adapted fluidly to whatever software application was running.

73
00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:29.120
[George] By introducing the App Store in 2008, Jobs created a global software economy that generated millions of jobs and revolutionized human social interaction, commerce, and media forever.

74
00:12:29.240 --> 00:12:38.520
[George] Diagnosed in 2003 with a rare neuroendocrine pancreatic cancer, Jobs faced his mortality with unvarnished philosophical courage.

75
00:12:38.520 --> 00:12:46.440
[George] In June 2005, delivering his landmark's Stanford University commencement address, Jobs urged graduates.

76
00:12:52.120 --> 00:12:53.560
[George] Stay foolish.

77
00:12:53.560 --> 00:13:00.440
[George] In 2010, Jobs unveiled the iPad, fulfilling his lifelong dream of an intimate digital canvas.

78
00:13:00.440 --> 00:13:11.720
[George] When Jobs passed away on October 5, 2011, at age 56, the entire globe mourned his passing with candlelight vigils outside Apple's stores from Tokyo to London.

79
00:13:11.720 --> 00:13:28.040
[George] Steve Jobs stood at the intersection of technology and the liberal arts, proving that uncompromising beauty, simplicity, and human intuition could elevate technology into high art, leaving an indelible dent in the universe that will shape human civilization for generations.

80
00:13:28.040 --> 00:13:43.640
[George] Jobs' serene stoicism in the face of terminal cancer gave his final public appearances an almost biblical gravitas, speaking to Stanford graduates about the importance of following one's heart and intuition, because death is the greatest change agent of life.

81
00:13:43.640 --> 00:13:57.320
[George] His introduction of the iPad in 2010 fulfilled his 30 year quest to create an intimate, magical pane of glass that allowed even toddlers and elderly grandparents to interact with digital information without technical friction.

82
00:13:57.320 --> 00:14:12.040
[George] Steve Jobs' enduring legacy is the profound truth that technology achieves its highest nobility when married to taste, art, and the human spirit, proving that one passionate individual can indeed put an everlasting dent in the universe.

83
00:14:25.840 --> 00:14:30.640
[Rebecca] Join us next week as Rebecca returns with the pioneering rocket dreams of Robert H.

84
00:14:30.640 --> 00:14:33.760
[Rebecca] Goddard, the father of modern astronautics.

85
00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:36.240
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

